course: ukrainian-street
lesson: 31
title: Olympiad: Cyrillic code-breaking and number puzzles ★★★
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: This olympiad lesson is a puzzle box: Ukrainian is written in Cyrillic letters, and like a UKLO puzzle you can crack its words from a few examples, with no dictionary at all.
explain: Some letters are old friends: А, О, М, Т and К sound just as they look. Others are tricksters: В is v, Н is n, Р is r, С is s and И is y — so НІ is not «hi» but ni, no!
explain: Some letters do the work of two English ones: Ш is sh, Ч is ch, Я is ya and Ю is yu — so БАБУСЯ has six letters, while babusya in English letters has seven.
explain: Numbers are puzzles too: -NAdtsyat works like «-teen», -dtsyat and -desyat like «-ty», and two odd ones, SOrok (40) and devyanOsto (90), break the pattern.
explain: Picture asking Sashko by text if he's coming to football and getting back «НІ»: an English eye reads «hi!», but he is saying no.
fact: Ukrainian is written in the Cyrillic alphabet, which has 33 letters.
fact: А, О, М, Т and К are said much like English a, o, m, t and k: МАМА is mama, ТАТО is tato and ТАК is tak, yes.
fact: Some Cyrillic letters look like English ones but stand for other sounds: В is v, Н is n, Р is r, С is s and Х is kh.
fact: И is written y in English letters and sounds like a short «ih», while І is written i and sounds like «ee»: ТРИ is try (three), СІМ is sim (seven).
fact: НІ is ni, no; an English reader may misread it as «hi», and ВИ is vy, the polite you, not «BI».
fact: One Cyrillic letter can stand for two English ones: Ш is sh, Ч is ch, Я is ya and Ю is yu.
fact: In Cyrillic the calling form changes only the last letter: МАМА → МАМО, ТАТО → ТАТУ, БАБУСЯ → БАБУСЮ.
fact: Number words in Cyrillic: ОДИН (odyn, 1), ДВА (dva, 2), ТРИ (try, 3), ЧОТИРИ (chotyry, 4), ШІСТЬ (shist, 6), СІМ (sim, 7), ВІСІМ (visim, 8), СТО (sto, 100); Ь is a small letter that softens the sound before it.
fact: The teens end in -nadtsyat: odyNAdtsyat 11, dvaNAdtsyat 12, trynAdtsyat 13, p'yatNAdtsyat 15, shistNAdtsyat 16.
fact: The tens: DVAdtsyat 20, TRYdtsyat 30, p'yatdesYAt 50, shistdesYAt 60, sImdesyat 70, vIsimdesyat 80; SOrok (40) and devyanOsto (90) are the odd ones, not built from their unit.
fact: Numbers like twenty-five are said ten first and then the unit, just as in English: DVAdtsyat p'yat.
fact: «ya tobI sto raziv kazAv!» means «I've told you a hundred times!»; it is a hyperbole meaning many times, not a count.
exercise: МАМА is mama and ТАТО is tato. How is tam (there) written in Cyrillic? | ТАМ | МАТ | ТОМ | ТАТ
exercise: You text Sashko «football?» and he sends back just «НІ». What does he mean? | No | Hi there, mate | Yes, coming | See you at four
exercise: Н is n and І is said «ee». Which Cyrillic word is ni, no? | НІ | ТАК | НО | ІН
exercise: How many letters has БАБУСЯ, and how many has babusya in English letters? | 6 and 7: Я does the work of y and a | 7 and 7: one letter for each sound | 6 and 6: the two are the same | 7 and 6: Cyrillic needs more
exercise: В looks like B but says v, and И says y. How do you read ВИ, the polite you? | vy | by | bi | ve
exercise: Which Cyrillic letter looks like English P but is said r? | Р | П | Б | Ф
exercise: С is s, Т is t and О is o. Read the number word СТО and say what it is. | sto, a hundred | sto, a thousand | cto, sixty | so, nothing at all
exercise: Odd one out: which number word does NOT start with the sound s? | ШІСТЬ, it starts with sh | СІМ, it has an І | СТО, it is the biggest | СОРОК, it is the oddest
exercise: odyNAdtsyat is 11, dvaNAdtsyat 12, p'yatNAdtsyat 15. What is shistNAdtsyat? | Sixteen | Sixty | Six | Sixty-six
exercise: Which of these tens is NOT built from its unit word? | SOrok, forty | TRYdtsyat, thirty | p'yatdesYAt, fifty | sImdesyat, seventy
exercise: DVAdtsyat take away sim. What is left? | trynAdtsyat, thirteen | DVAdtsyat sim, twenty-seven | dvaNAdtsyat, twelve | TRYdtsyat, thirty
exercise: Sashko counts in tens: «DVAdtsyat, TRYdtsyat, …, p'yatdesYAt». What goes in the gap? | SOrok | chotyrdesYAt | chotyRY | chotyrNAdtsyat
exercise: How is twenty-five said in Ukrainian? | DVAdtsyat p'yat | p'yat DVAdtsyat | dvaNAdtsyat p'yat | p'yatdesYAt dva
exercise: When you call her, МАМА becomes МАМО. Which letter changed? | The last one: А became О | The first one: М became Н | Both А letters became О | None, it is just said louder
exercise: «ya tobI sto raziv kazAv!» shouts Mum. How many times did she really say it? | Many times, nobody counted | Exactly a hundred times | Seven times, one a day | Just once, this morning
exercise: p'yatdesYAt take away p'yatNAdtsyat. What is the answer? | TRYdtsyat p'yat | SOrok | DVAdtsyat p'yat | nol
test: «lol my cousin's text says ВІСІМ, that's 'BICIM', some English word innit» What does it really say? | visim, eight | bicim, a kind of bike | sim, seven | vis, eighty
test: «Cyrillic is just English letters flipped round, ez» says your mate. Check it. | Wrong: some look English but sound different, like В for v | Right: every Cyrillic letter is an English one turned round | Right: Cyrillic and English are exactly the same alphabet | Wrong: Cyrillic has no letters at all, only little pictures
test: Sashko has vIsim sweets and you have shIst. You pile them up and dIlymo navpIl. How many each? | sim | vIsim | shIst | chotyrNAdtsyat
test: «bruv I counted to forty, that's chotyRY tens, so it's 'chotyrdesyat' innit» Is he right? | No: forty is its own odd word, SOrok, not built from chotyRY | Yes, chotyrdesyat is the proper word for forty | No, forty is p'yatdesYAt, said like fifty | No, forty is devyanOsto, the odd one
test: A kid writes «ДВА ДІВЧИНИ» (dva DIvchyny, two girls). What should he fix? | ДВА → ДВІ: it's dvi before DIvchyny | Nothing at all, it is spot on | ДВА → ТРИ: girls go in threes | ДІВЧИНИ → ДІВЧИНА: there is one girl
test: A puzzle card says ТАТО → ТАТУ!, МАМА → МАМО!. A girl is called ОКСАНА. How do you call her? | ОКСАНО! | ОКСАНУ! | ОКСАНА! | ОКСАНЮ!
test: «ugh this hide and seek took like a million years, he counted to sto twice» How far did the seeker really count in all? | Two hundred | A million, like she says | One hundred | Two thousand
test: Odd one out: ДВА, ТРИ, ТАК, СТО — and why? | ТАК: it means yes, so it is not a number at all | ДВА: it is the only one with a В | ТРИ: it is the only one with an И | СТО: it is the biggest of the four
test: «nah mate, СІМ is 'sim' like a SIM card, it means phone» Right? | No: СІМ is sim, seven | Yes, it means a phone card | No, it is shIst, six | No, it is sto, a hundred
test: ЧОТИРИ is chotyry. Which letter does the job of ch? | Ч | О | Т | И
test: «we lost a bit lol» says Sashko. The score was nol — DEsyat. How bad was it really? | Very bad: ten goals to none | Close: just one goal in it | Not bad at all: it was a draw | Great: they won by ten clear goals
test: «TRYdtsyat and trynAdtsyat, same thing, both thirteen» says your mate. Which is right? | No: TRYdtsyat is 30, trynAdtsyat 13 | Yes, both mean thirteen | No, both of them mean thirty | No: TRYdtsyat is just 3
exam: Determine the number of Cyrillic letters in the word БАБУСЯ, given that Я represents the sounds ya. | Six | Seven | Five | Eight
exam: Identify the numeral which, among the tens from twenty to eighty, is alone in not being formed from the corresponding unit. | SOrok, forty, a word of its own | TRYdtsyat, thirty | sImdesyat, seventy | DVAdtsyat, twenty
exam: Determine the reading of the Cyrillic word НІ, which resembles an English greeting. | ni, signifying no | hi, a greeting | ni, signifying yes | ny, signifying now
exam: Compute shistdesYAt less shistNAdtsyat and express the result in Ukrainian. | SOrok chotyRY, forty-four | p'yatdesYAt, fifty | SOrok, forty | trynAdtsyat, thirteen
